Relevant tables / columns and why they matter

lap_positions  
• race_id – filter to one race at a time.  
• driver_id – whose position it is.  
• lap – allows looking at lap N and lap N‑1.  
• position – ordering of the car on that lap.  
• lap_type – tells whether the record is “Race”, “Starting Position”, “Pit Entry”, “Pit Exit”, etc.; needed to ignore / re‑classify non‑race positions.

pit_stops  
• race_id, driver_id – join to lap_positions.  
• lap – lap on which the stop happened (entry).  
• stop – 1‑based stop number per driver; useful for detecting first‑vs‑second stop.  
• milliseconds / duration – not needed for counting, but lets you tag “pit‑in” / “pit‑out”.

retirements  
• race_id, driver_id – who retired.  
• lap – lap on which the retirement occurred.  
• retirement_type – used to tag “retirement overtake”.

results  
• race_id, driver_id, grid – starting grid order.  
• position (final) – check start‑lap vs grid for “race start” overtakes.

status  
• status_id, status – combine with results.status_id when you need final DNF info.

races / races_ext  
• race_id, year – put counts in historical context or restrict to modern era.

drivers / drivers_ext (driver names) and constructors* – purely for presentable labels once counts are known.

Conditions / filters typically used

• Consider only races that have at least one row in pit_stops:  
  WHERE race_id IN (SELECT DISTINCT race_id FROM pit_stops)

• Work only on ordinary race laps:  
  lap_positions.lap_type = 'Race'

• Detect pairwise position swaps between lap N‑1 and lap N:  
  JOIN lap_positions AS p0  ON …  AND p0.lap = p1.lap‑1  
  WHERE p0.position <  p0_other.position    -- driver A ahead of B on lap N‑1  
    AND p1.position >  p1_other.position    -- driver A now behind B on lap N

• Exclude pit‑lane artefacts when classifying on‑track overtakes:  
  NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pit_stops … WHERE … lap = p1.lap)

• Tag pit‑entry / pit‑exit events:  
  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pit_stops … WHERE … lap = p0.lap  )  -- entry  
  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pit_stops … WHERE … lap = p0.lap‑1)  -- exit

• Tag retirement events:  
  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM retirements r  
          WHERE r.race_id = p1.race_id AND r.driver_id = ahead.driver_id  
            AND r.lap = p1.lap)

Ten exploratory SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows)

```sql
-- 1. Races that actually have pit‑stop data
SELECT DISTINCT race_id
FROM pit_stops
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct lap_type values – see what the data calls pit entries / exits
SELECT DISTINCT lap_type
FROM lap_positions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect lap‑by‑lap ordering for one sample race that has pit data
SELECT lap, driver_id, position
FROM lap_positions
WHERE race_id = 841          -- change to any race_id returned by query #1
  AND lap_type = 'Race'
ORDER BY lap, position
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Show all pit‑stops inside that sample race
SELECT driver_id, lap, stop, duration
FROM pit_stops
WHERE race_id = 841
ORDER BY driver_id, lap
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Show drivers that retired in the sample race
SELECT driver_id, lap, retirement_type
FROM retirements
WHERE race_id = 841
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Grid vs first‑race‑lap ordering → possible “race‑start” overtakes
SELECT g.driver_id,
       g.grid             AS grid_pos,
       p.position         AS lap1_pos
FROM results  AS g
JOIN lap_positions AS p
  ON p.race_id = g.race_id
 AND p.driver_id = g.driver_id
WHERE g.race_id = 841
  AND p.lap      = 1
  AND p.lap_type = 'Race'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Raw candidate on‑track overtakes between lap N‑1 and lap N
SELECT p1.race_id,
       p1.lap,
       behind.driver_id  AS behind_driver,
       ahead.driver_id   AS passed_driver
FROM lap_positions AS p1
JOIN lap_positions AS behind
  ON behind.race_id = p1.race_id
 AND behind.lap     = p1.lap
JOIN lap_positions AS p0              -- same driver, previous lap
  ON p0.race_id    = behind.race_id
 AND p0.driver_id  = behind.driver_id
 AND p0.lap        = behind.lap - 1
JOIN lap_positions AS ahead           -- driver who was ahead on lap N‑1
  ON ahead.race_id = p0.race_id
 AND ahead.lap     = p0.lap
 AND ahead.position = p0.position - 1
JOIN lap_positions AS ahead_now       -- same “ahead” driver on lap N
  ON ahead_now.race_id  = ahead.race_id
 AND ahead_now.driver_id = ahead.driver_id
 AND ahead_now.lap       = ahead.lap + 1
WHERE p1.lap_type = 'Race'
  AND ahead_now.position > behind.position
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add a pit‑stop test: overtakes occurring on same lap as a pit entry
SELECT o.*
FROM (
  /* copy of query #7 without LIMIT */
  SELECT p1.race_id,
         p1.lap,
         behind.driver_id  AS behind_driver,
         ahead.driver_id   AS passed_driver
  FROM lap_positions AS p1
  JOIN lap_positions AS behind
    ON behind.race_id = p1.race_id
   AND behind.lap     = p1.lap
  JOIN lap_positions AS p0
    ON p0.race_id    = behind.race_id
   AND p0.driver_id  = behind.driver_id
   AND p0.lap        = behind.lap - 1
  JOIN lap_positions AS ahead
    ON ahead.race_id = p0.race_id
   AND ahead.lap     = p0.lap
   AND ahead.position = p0.position - 1
  JOIN lap_positions AS ahead_now
    ON ahead_now.race_id  = ahead.race_id
   AND ahead_now.driver_id = ahead.driver_id
   AND ahead_now.lap       = ahead.lap + 1
  WHERE p1.lap_type = 'Race'
    AND ahead_now.position > behind.position
) AS o
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1
              FROM pit_stops ps
              WHERE ps.race_id = o.race_id
                AND ps.driver_id = o.passed_driver   -- car that gets passed pits
                AND ps.lap = o.lap)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Overtakes triggered by a retirement on that lap
SELECT o.*
FROM (
  /* same base detection of swaps as above */
  SELECT p1.race_id,
         p1.lap,
         behind.driver_id  AS behind_driver,
         ahead.driver_id   AS passed_driver
  FROM lap_positions AS p1
  JOIN lap_positions AS behind
    ON behind.race_id = p1.race_id
   AND behind.lap     = p1.lap
  JOIN lap_positions AS p0
    ON p0.race_id    = behind.race_id
   AND p0.driver_id  = behind.driver_id
   AND p0.lap        = behind.lap - 1
  JOIN lap_positions AS ahead
    ON ahead.race_id = p0.race_id
   AND ahead.lap     = p0.lap
   AND ahead.position = p0.position - 1
  JOIN lap_positions AS ahead_now
    ON ahead_now.race_id  = ahead.race_id
   AND ahead_now.driver_id = ahead.driver_id
   AND ahead_now.lap       = ahead.lap + 1
  WHERE p1.lap_type = 'Race'
    AND ahead_now.position > behind.position
) AS o
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1
              FROM retirements r
              WHERE r.race_id = o.race_id
                AND r.driver_id = o.passed_driver
                AND r.lap = o.lap)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Count overtakes by coarse type across the whole database
WITH
base AS (   -- all position‑swap events
  SELECT p1.race_id,
         p1.lap,
         behind.driver_id  AS behind_driver,
         ahead.driver_id   AS passed_driver
  FROM lap_positions AS p1
  JOIN lap_positions AS behind
    ON behind.race_id = p1.race_id
   AND behind.lap     = p1.lap
  JOIN lap_positions AS p0
    ON p0.race_id    = behind.race_id
   AND p0.driver_id  = behind.driver_id
   AND p0.lap        = behind.lap - 1
  JOIN lap_positions AS ahead
    ON ahead.race_id = p0.race_id
   AND ahead.lap     = p0.lap
   AND ahead.position = p0.position - 1
  JOIN lap_positions AS ahead_now
    ON ahead_now.race_id  = ahead.race_id
   AND ahead_now.driver_id = ahead.driver_id
   AND ahead_now.lap       = ahead.lap + 1
  WHERE p1.lap_type = 'Race'
    AND ahead_now.position > behind.position
    AND p1.race_id IN (SELECT DISTINCT race_id FROM pit_stops)  -- races w/ pit data
),
classified AS (
  SELECT b.*,
         CASE
           WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pit_stops ps
                        WHERE ps.race_id = b.race_id
                          AND ps.driver_id = b.passed_driver
                          AND ps.lap = b.lap)            THEN 'Pit‑Entry Pass'
           WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pit_stops ps
                        WHERE ps.race_id = b.race_id
                          AND ps.driver_id = b.passed_driver
                          AND ps.lap = b.lap - 1)        THEN 'Pit‑Exit Pass'
           WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM retirements r
                        WHERE r.race_id = b.race_id
                          AND r.driver_id = b.passed_driver
                          AND r.lap = b.lap)              THEN 'Retirement Pass'
           ELSE 'On‑Track Pass'
         END AS overtake_type
  FROM base b
)
SELECT overtake_type,
       COUNT(*) AS num_overtakes
FROM classified
GROUP BY overtake_type
ORDER BY num_overtakes DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progress from simple inspection of required data (queries 1‑6), through basic detection of position swaps (query 7), to the inclusion of pit‑stop and retirement logic (queries 8‑9), and finally to a complete classification and count of each overtake type across all races with pit‑stop information (query 10).
